  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates the Aharonov–Bohm phase effect. A charged particle, shown as an orange sphere, travels from left to right along two alternative red trajectories that split and later recombine. Between these paths sits a vertical blue cylindrical region carrying magnetic flux, marked by upward arrows and the symbol Φ. The particle paths curve around this flux tube rather than going through it, emphasizing that the important effect comes from enclosing the flux.\n\nThe main point is that even if the particle moves in regions where the magnetic field may be absent or confined inside the cylinder, the two routes acquire different quantum phases. This phase difference is indicated by the formula Δφ = eΦ/ħ. The image therefore conveys that the interference observed when the paths meet again depends directly on the enclosed magnetic flux.\n\nVisually, the comparison is between the upper and lower routes around the central flux: they are geometrically different in how they wind around the cylinder, and that difference produces a measurable phase shift. The figure is a compact schematic of how electromagnetic potentials influence quantum motion through interference.",
